A||||6||ChadMan||If you have ever removed an 88 steering rack PLEASE read||||5||
Z||000000||ChadMan||09-10-2001||10:32 PM||||I'm having second thoughts about turning my 88 Coupe over to Tuffy...I keep having nightmarish visions of them calling me midway through the job and wanting $750 to replace the steering rack.<P>So I re-read people's previous posts, took out my spare tire tub, and saw how easy it really is to get to the rack bolts...and with the rack out, it should be easy to replace the inner tie rod ends.  ("SHOULD be" = famous last words!)<P>I have two questions:<P>1)  The rack-to-subframe bolts thread into nuts that are hidden in a U-shaped bracket.  Are these nuts tack-welded to the bracket on the 88 (other posters said they are on the 84-87)?  Because when I started to turn one bolt, I could tell it was going to shear off, and I'm wondering what the hell I'll do then...<P>2)  If the bolts break off and I have to drill them out and remove the nuts, how the hell do I get a wrench down in the U-shaped bracket to hold the nut when I install a new bolt?<P>Thank you VERY much for any info.  To be honest, as much as I love my Fiero, I am getting really sick of dicking around with it.||||reg||5
Z||000001||skitime||09-10-2001||10:47 PM||||Removing the rack is really no different in the 88 and you can do it yourself.  The clamps are not welded to the frame.  Go for it!<P>------------------<BR><UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>Red 88 GT T-TOP<BR><LI>Red 86 GT<BR></UL>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature-1.JPG">  <p>[This message has been edited by skitime (edited 09-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||DRH||09-10-2001||11:56 PM||||Chadman,<P>The nuts are welded on. I managed to get all mine out. One did break off but I heated it with a torch and was able to get it out with vise grips. I would try heating them first and see if you can get the bolts out without breaking them. <P>It's possible to replace inner tie rods with the rack in if you want to go that route. <P>Also, order an aluminum rack bushing from Rodney Dickman <A HREF="http://www.rodneydickman.com." TARGET=_blank>www.rodneydickman.com.</A>  The factory one is plastic and is probably worn out. You can still get the plastic ones fron GM but personally I'd recommend the aluminum one.<P>Good luck.<P><p>[This message has been edited by DRH (edited 09-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||DRH||09-11-2001||12:05 AM||||If you do wind up having to drill them out, I would try using a drill just big enough to get the bolt out and try re-tapping to get the rest of it out. I wouldn't remove the nuts unless all else fails.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Leadfoot||09-11-2001||12:17 AM||||If one of the upper bolts break you can use a rachet equipped with an appropriate extension and swivel to break the welded nuts loose from the back.  Then you can feed a bolt from the back side and bolt up the steering rack.  This worked for me.||||reg||1
Z||000005||ChadMan||09-11-2001||01:38 PM||||You guys are great.  I had no luck getting the old inners off with the rack in the car, but armed with the tips from you guys, I think I will try removing the rack this weekend.<P>I already have the Dickman bushing...it will be a breeze to install with the rack out of the car.<P>Thanks!||||reg||14
Z||000006||Fiero_24||09-11-2001||01:44 PM||||Talk to Oreif about how to take it off the car.  He helped me with my cracked rack.  Just watch out for the bolts cracking, one of mine did.  He still finished the job in less than 5 hours, and most of the time was drilling out the snapped bolt.  I was quoted $750 also, but if you can do it yourself, DO IT.<P>Craig<P>------------------<BR>  [<A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24</A> <BR>1987 GT||||reg||1
